1.3  How virtual memory works

TL;DR
  - Every process sees its own memory, as if it were alone: the addresses it
    uses are virtual.
  - The CPU's MMU translates them into real RAM through page tables the kernel
    keeps, one page (usually 4 KiB) at a time.
  - Memory is only handed out when first touched: the first access to a page
    causes a page fault, and the kernel maps a page then.

      cat /proc/self/maps | head            # a process's memory map
      grep -E '^pg(maj)?fault ' /proc/vmstat

On your machine
  Page      4.0 KiB
  Faults    809.6 million served from RAM, 2.4 million that read the disk, since
            boot
  Swap      15.6 MiB read back, 256.0 MiB written out
  Huge pages transparent huge pages: madvise

Pitfalls
  - Page faults are normal: most are minor, served from RAM. Major faults read
    the disk, and those are the slow ones.
  - VSZ (or VIRT) in ps and top is reserved address space, not RAM in use: look
    at RSS (RES).
  - A segmentation fault is an access to an address the process has no mapping
    for, such as a null pointer: the kernel sends it SIGSEGV.
  - Shared libraries count in the RSS of every process using them, but sit in
    RAM once: adding up RSS overstates. PSS in smaps splits them fairly.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why not let programs use real addresses?

    Isolation first: a process can't read or break another's memory. Then
    freedom for the kernel: it can place pages anywhere, share identical ones
    (libraries, or a process and its fork until one writes), move rarely used
    ones to swap, and give every program the same simple layout.

  ▾ What are the TLB and huge pages?

    Reading the page tables on every memory access would be slow, so the CPU
    keeps recent translations in a small cache, the TLB. With 4 KiB pages, a big
    program needs many entries; a huge page (2 MiB on x86-64) covers 512 times
    more with one. Transparent huge pages let the kernel use them by itself:
    always, only where a program asks (madvise), or never.

Related
  Commands  ps, top
  Paths     /proc/PID/maps, /proc/PID/smaps, /proc/vmstat
  Hub       atfq memory Memory
  See also  atfq 17.4 The page cache
  See also  atfq 17.3 Swap and swappiness
